Commvault Deduplication Database (DDB) sizing formulas

1. Core DDB Sizing Formula (Rule of Thumb)
Primary Formula

DDB Size = 2–4% of the total protected front-end data

2% → Low change rate, good dedup, mostly incremental

4% → High change rate, many clients, aggressive retention

Example

100 TB protected data

DDB size = 2–4 TB

2. More Accurate Formula (Change-Rate Based)

This is closer to what Commvault architects actually use:

DDB Size ≈ Unique Data × 3–5%

Where:

Unique Data = data after deduplication (back-end size)

Example

Front-end data: 100 TB

Dedup ratio: 10:1

Unique data: 10 TB

DDB size ≈ 300–500 GB

This explains why good dedup ratios dramatically reduce DDB growth.

3. Daily Growth Estimation Formula

Useful for capacity planning:

Daily DDB Growth ≈ Daily Changed Data × 3–5%

Example

Daily change rate: 1 TB

Daily DDB growth: 30–50 GB/day

4. Client-Side vs MediaAgent-Side Dedup Impact
Dedup Type	DDB Growth	Notes
Client-Side	Slightly higher	More fingerprints generated
MediaAgent-Side	Slightly lower	Centralized processing
5. Streams and DDB Sizing Relationship

High stream counts increase:

Fingerprint generation

DDB lookup activity

Rule of Thumb:

Increase DDB size by 10–20% for high-stream or high-concurrency environments

6. DDB Disk Performance Requirements (Sizing Adjacent)

Even a correctly sized DDB will fail if performance is poor.

Recommended:

SSD or NVMe only

Dedicated filesystem

Low latency (<5 ms preferred)

Rough IOPS Guideline:

~50–75 IOPS per active backup stream

7. When to Split DDBs (Very Important)

You should create multiple DDBs when:

DDB exceeds 10–15 TB

Mixed workloads (VMs + DB dumps + file servers)

Dedup ratio drops unexpectedly

MediaAgent CPU consistently high

8. DDB Protection Sizing (Don’t Forget This)

You must also size for:

DDB backups

DASH copies

Reconstruction overhead

Add 10–20% overhead to your calculated DDB size.


Front-end sizing:   2–4%
Unique data sizing: 3–5%
Daily growth:       Change rate × 3–5%
Disk:               SSD/NVMe only
Split DDBs:         >10–15 TB
